WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.040
Hey Crazies.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:07.000
There this thing that mathematicians and physicists
like to say:

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.400
Math is the language of the universe.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.870
You’ve probably heard it so many times that
you’ve just accepted it as truth.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:17.180
But rule #8 here at the Science Asylum is
Question Everything?

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.920
Is math really the language of the universe?

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.460
Answering this question is going to take some
time and we need to be careful,

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.280
so let’s start with a simpler question:

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.760
Is math even a language?

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:28.180
What is a language, anyway?

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:30.260
That’s two questions, but I’ll see what
I can do.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:33.880
We need to know what a language is before
we can decide if math is one.

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:39.129
A Language is a complex system of words and
symbols, either spoken or written, used by

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:42.340
a particular community as a means of communication.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:45.840
OK, so it makes sense why something like English,
German, Arabic,

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.900
or even iglatin pay would be languages.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:48.940
But what about math?

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:51.860
Is math a complex system of words and symbols?

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:52.700
Yes!

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:54.140
Either spoken or written?

00:00:54.140 --> 00:00:55.840
Mostly written, but yes!

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.839
Is it used by a particular community?

00:00:57.840 --> 00:00:59.200
Definitely, yes!

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:00.920
As a means of communication?

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:02.400
Well… maybe?

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.700
The purpose of math isn’t just to communicate
ideas to each other.

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.860
Math is used as a tool to make predictions
about the actual world.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.020
Of course, this requires us to also ask:

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:14.680
Is the sole purpose of a language to communicate?

00:01:14.680 --> 00:01:17.500
I would say no… and I think most of you
would agree with me.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.780
Language can also be used a tool.

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.100
In the right hands, language can be used to
inspire, to lightening the mood,

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.120
or to strengthen someone’s resolve.

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.340
In the wrong hands, it can be used to hurt
someone, to destroy their reputation,

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.900
instill fear, or even to control others.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.300
I don’t think the fact that math is used
as a tool is a disqualifier.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.360
The definition of a language doesn’t even
require that communication be the sole purpose.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.180
It said “as a means of” not “for the
purpose of”

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:46.040
I’m still not entirely convinced though.

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:47.260
We need another test.

00:01:47.260 --> 00:01:50.439
Something else we know about languages is
that you can translate between them,

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:54.040
so, if we can translate between math and a
language like English,

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:55.600
then we might have a decent argument.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:57.480
Oh! You want start an argument? Well, let me tell you...

00:01:57.480 --> 00:01:59.000
No, not that kind of argument.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:03.060
When I say “Argument,” I don’t mean
one of those fights over politics or whatever.

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:08.040
An argument is more of a set of reasons or
ideas in support of a conclusion

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:11.120
in an effort to persuade others to the same
conclusion.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.480
It’s a point made by a logical individual
in a calm discussion.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:18.300
People are rarely thinking logically in political
conversations.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.280
Anyway, let’s give translation a try.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:23.880
When you translate from any language to another,
some meanings can get lost,

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:26.880
but as long as most ideas can translate, I
think we’re good.

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:30.380
We said earlier that languages are made of
words and symbols

00:02:30.380 --> 00:02:32.680
and that math has plenty of symbols.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.560
The most recognizable symbol is probably this
one: the equal sign.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.300
The word “equal” is more of a name than
a meaning though.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:43.340
A direct translation for the equal sign would
be “is the same as”

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:46.000
which is a phrase rather than a word, but
that’s OK.

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.680
Translations don’t have to be a one-word-to-one-word
correspondence.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:55.160
This English 8-word sentence is 6 words in
Polish, but 10 words in Albanian.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:57.300
Math is just much more concise.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:59.720
It takes way fewer symbols to contain a meaning.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:03.160
Except you don’t find a mathematics option
in Google Translate

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.020
So what gives?

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.160
Is it a language or not?

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:07.940
Hmm, I don’t think we can judge by Google
Translate.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:13.200
It only has 103 languages
and there are over 6900 spoken languages currently

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:16.040
in use,
not to mention all the dead languages.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:18.940
My point is that Google Translate is far from
complete,

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.780
so let’s try some translations on our own.

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:23.560
Math uses the Latin letters and the Greek
letters

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.810
both upper case and lower case
and a whole collection of other unique symbols;

00:03:27.810 --> 00:03:30.380
which is probably why people are so afraid
of it.

00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:34.700
All of those symbols have at least one meaning,
but many have multiple meanings that we have

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:36.440
to understand by context.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.240
For example, an upper case “B” could be
the bulk elastic constant,

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:44.120
which represents how rigid something is in
response to pressure changes.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:47.400
Like air in response to a sound wave passing
through it.

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:50.980
But an upper case “B” could also be the
strength of a magnetic field

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:53.820
the kind of thing you might imagine is around
a bar magnet.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:55.800
This is pretty typical of languages though.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:57.800
Take the English word “Duck,” for example.

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:01.900
It could refer to a type of bird
or the action of suddenly lowering your head.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.840
Words with multiple meanings are so common
there’s a word to describe them: Homonym,

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.980
so multiple meanings doesn’t disqualify
math as a language either.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:11.620
Let’s try to translate a whole sentence.

00:04:11.620 --> 00:04:14.840
Say you’ve got a simple mathematical sentence
like this.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:21.300
It makes use of 6 symbols: Delta, s, Equals,
f, i, and minus.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.760
We’ve already seen Equals means “is the
same as.”

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.720
Delta means “change in” and S means “position”
or “where something is.”

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:32.200
The “f” and “i” are only subscripts.

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:34.560
They just describe the symbol they’re attached
to,

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.660
so they’re more like adjectives than nouns.

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:39.380
“f” stands for “final” or “at the
end,”

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:42.280
“i” stands for “initial” or “at
the beginning,”

00:04:42.280 --> 00:04:44.540
and minus means “the difference between.”

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:50.080
So this mathematical sentence reads: “The
change in where something is is the same as

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:53.700
the difference between where that something
is at the end and where that something is

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:54.740
at the beginning.”

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.540
Seems like a pretty logical statement.

00:04:56.540 --> 00:04:58.960
You can do this with other mathematical sentences
too.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.360
Newton’s 2nd Law, Ohm’s Law, even Einstein’s
Equation.

00:05:02.360 --> 00:05:04.820
Like any other translation though, you lose
some meaning.

00:05:04.820 --> 00:05:08.319
Mathematical sentences say a lot more than
their English translation.

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.580
You’re also going to have a really hard
time translating the other way.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:14.560
This English sentence has no translation in
Math.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:17.280
Although, I’m willing to hear arguments
in favor of this one.

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.460
Translations, Grammar, Adjectives, and Nouns.

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:21.880
Sounds a lot like a language to me.

00:05:21.880 --> 00:05:25.040
And, as a scientist, I use it to communicate
with people all the time.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:26.200
So is math a language?

00:05:26.210 --> 00:05:28.889
I would argue “yes”
and, just like any other language, it’s

00:05:28.889 --> 00:05:30.200
governed by a set of rules.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.280
Those rules are just a lot more absolute than
a typical language

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:34.760
and they’re built from the ground up.

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:37.640
This gives math some extra power if you know
how to use it,

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.660
which brings us back to our original question:

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:42.139
Is math really the language of the universe?

00:05:42.139 --> 00:05:43.979
OK, this one is a little trickier.

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:48.540
If math really is the language of the universe,
then anything we calculate should have meaning,

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.180
which seems to be true of counting.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:55.349
We take something like this and call it “one.”
and something like this and call it “two.”

00:05:55.349 --> 00:05:57.919
We can put them together and call that “three.”

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:00.940
We can put three of those threes together
and call it “nine”

00:06:00.940 --> 00:06:04.980
These seem like pretty solid rules
and they seem to apply to the universe.

00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:08.080
But we’re going to learn a lot more if we
try to disprove it.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:10.780
We need to find a number that doesn’t have
any meaning.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:13.370
Hmm, there are tons of calculations I could
just make up,

00:06:13.370 --> 00:06:15.729
but no one would ever expect those to have
meaning anyway.

00:06:15.729 --> 00:06:17.920
Maybe something people would expect to have
meaning.

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:18.900
What about density?

00:06:18.900 --> 00:06:20.080
Oh! That’s brilliant!

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:21.680
I’m so glad you’re a clone of me.

00:06:21.680 --> 00:06:24.840
Density measures the “compactness” of
a substance.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:28.870
An easy way to calculate it is take the mass
and divide by the volume

00:06:28.870 --> 00:06:32.310
and this tells you something about the spacing
between the molecules.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:36.529
It makes sense for things like ice or rubber
or helium gas or air

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:37.440
Wait a minute!

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:40.600
Density measures the “compactness” of
a substance.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:42.960
Air is a mixture of a bunch of different things.

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:46.120
Mostly Nitrogen and Oxygen gas, but still a
mixture.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:48.900
It’s a pretty uniform mixture though, so
I could understand

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:51.820
arguments for calling that mixture one
substance.

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:53.180
But what about this?

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:56.240
Can’t I just measure mass on scale
and the volume using a beaker

00:06:56.240 --> 00:06:57.700
and then divide the two numbers?

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.660
I suppose you could, but that number wouldn’t
really say anything.

00:07:01.660 --> 00:07:05.440
It’s what we’d call an average density,
which might make sense for a uniform mixture

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:06.420
like air.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:07.720
But not for this rubber ducky.

00:07:07.720 --> 00:07:10.840
It’s made of two substances: rubber and
air.

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:13.340
and they’re definitely not uniform throughout
the ducky.

00:07:13.340 --> 00:07:16.660
So, while it might be a useful short-cut in
a calculation somewhere,

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:19.759
it doesn’t actually tell you anything about
the compactness of the ducky.

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:21.980
It’s a number that only exists on paper.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:24.580
It has no physical meaning
and that’s another reason it can’t be

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:28.099
used to explain why things float,
commenters!!

00:07:28.100 --> 00:07:31.220
Anyway, all average values sit in this grey
area.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.660
Sometimes they have meaning and sometimes
they don’t.

00:07:33.660 --> 00:07:36.120
Meaning is what we’re looking for in science.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:40.140
We’re trying to answer deep philosophical
questions about our existence like:

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:41.260
Where did we come from?

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:42.380
Why are we here?

00:07:42.380 --> 00:07:44.460
Science is about meaning.

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.160
It’s about language.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.320
Plus there are plenty of statements that have
meaning,

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:50.340
but don’t have a translation into math.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.640
Math alone will never be enough.

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:56.220
No number will ever answer those deep philosophical
questions.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:57.460
Not even 42!

00:07:57.460 --> 00:08:00.160
Math is not the language of the universe.

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:04.060
Even if we wanted to personify the universe
and say it’s trying to communicate with

00:08:04.069 --> 00:08:06.249
a language,
which I would advise against,

00:08:06.249 --> 00:08:10.660
Math is only a small part of our best translation
of it.

00:08:10.660 --> 00:08:11.509
So, what do you think?

00:08:11.509 --> 00:08:13.060
Does the universe work using math?

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:14.439
Or is it something we made up?

00:08:14.440 --> 00:08:15.700
I’d like to know in the comments.

00:08:15.700 --> 00:08:17.420
Thanks for liking and sharing this video…

00:08:17.420 --> 00:08:19.460
Don’t forget to subscribe if you'd like to keep up with us.

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:22.740
And until next time, remember, it’s OK to
be a little crazy.

00:08:25.000 --> 00:08:27.220
In the last video, we learned about the lives
of stars.

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:28.639
Comment response time!

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:32.980
Benbedra mentioned you can learn a lot more
from star light than just color and age.

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:36.180
Yes, using optics, we can also learn what
the star is made of

00:08:36.180 --> 00:08:38.500
and how fast it’s moving toward or away
from us.

00:08:38.500 --> 00:08:42.810
You can also get distance sometimes,
but that’s a topic for another video.

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:47.100
Ira Sanborn told his kids the light coming
from the Sun peaks in green!

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:48.280
It’s pretty weird, but it’s true.

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:49.950
In fact, it’s very common for stars.

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.470
If you take all the peak wavelengths from
all the stars in the universe,

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:54.860
you get a greenish color.

00:08:54.860 --> 00:08:57.940
It just doesn’t look green because it’s
emitting the other colors too.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:02.560
When our eyes receive light of all colors,
it sends that information to the brain,

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:06.280
but that just looks like a jumbled mess,
so your brain turns it all white to create

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:08.100
contrast in your field-of-view.

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:10.200
Yeah, white light is an illusion.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:11.700
Also a topic for another video.

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.780
Miss Physics wished she had studied more 
astrophysics in her undergrad.

00:09:14.780 --> 00:09:18.520
The downside to university programs
is really all the non-majors courses you have to

00:09:18.520 --> 00:09:19.860
take to get a degree.

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:24.080
Undergrad took me 5 years and I still didn’t get
to take all the undegrad physics courses I should have.

00:09:24.080 --> 00:09:25.780
I guess we’ll just have to learn things
on our own.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:28.320
It’s more fun that way anyway. School's intense.

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.940
Graeme Wilson asked if black holes were frozen
at absolute zero

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:33.200
and the answer is “no.”

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:36.420
For a while, we had special laws for black
holes,

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:39.320
but eventually realized they were just the
laws of thermodynamics.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:43.120
Black holes have to obey the laws of thermodynamics
just like anything else.

00:09:43.120 --> 00:09:45.280
Even though that might result in a few paradoxes.

00:09:45.280 --> 00:09:46.360
Future video.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.300
I agree completely with Jim Groth that how
we know what we know is very important.

00:09:50.300 --> 00:09:54.860
Getting to see the process can make science
feel more doable to non-scientists.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:59.580
And learning the history behind it can help us
understand the obstacles we ran into along the way,

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.780
which is why my advanced eBook is written
from a historical perspective.

00:10:02.780 --> 00:10:06.120
Because even people who go into physics like
me need that history.

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:10.260
Keadin Mode Star Trek is the best the
reason to talk about Wolf 359!

00:10:10.260 --> 00:10:14.100
A close second would be that it’s so low-mass,
it almost isn’t a star.

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.500
